Introduction

  • The 12V-2x6 (12VHPWR) connectors are notorious for their flammability.
  • The ASUS ROG ASTRAL implementation of RTX 5090 monitors voltage and current
    flowing through connector pins.

  This project provides a native Linux implementation of the current monitoring
  functionality of ASUS ROG ASTRAL devices. The support is currently limited to
  ROG-ASTRAL-RTX5090-O32G and ROG-ASTRAL-RTX5090-O32G-WHITE devices.

  • The bin/susm binary implements a simple monitor.
  • The bin/susd binary implements a simple daemon that attempts to reduce the
    power draw when it detects either overload on any of the connectors pins or
    when there is a significant mismatch between individual wires.

Considerations

  The specification requires the wires to be rated for at least 9.2 A current.
  The RTX 5090 cards are rated for a maximal power draw of 600 W. With balanced
  distribution of load across the wires this amounts to 8.5 A current per wire.

  Faulty connections, wires, and connectors can move the load distribution out
  of balance, overloading some of the wires. To counter these in live systems
  it is possible to limit the power draw of the card. 

  • The power management facilities of the RTX 5090 permit dynamic setting
    between 400 and 600 W. Within this range the card adjusts its clocks to 
    meet the power limits.
  • Outside of this range the power can be further limited by adjusting the
    clocks manually, making it possible to further reduce the power draw down
    to approximately 60 W.

  When reducing the power there are two strategies possible.

  • If the target power limit lies within the (400, 600) range, use the power
    management facilities directly.
  • If it lies below, adjust the clocks manually.

  Control algorithm

  • Compute rate := max(current) / threshold
  • Compute goal := powerLimitUpper * rate
  • If (powerLimitLower < goal) {
    ... use nvml to limit the power,
    else
    ... take current clock frequency and reduce it by a step.

